          sampleAnswer: "`restrict` tells the implementation that, for a particular execution of a block, accesses through a restrict-qualified pointer are intended to be the relevant way of accessing the referenced objects. When the programmer satisfies that contract, the compiler can use it to optimize alias analysis. Violating the restrict contract can produce undefined behavior, so it should only be used when the aliasing assumptions are true.",
          detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\n`restrict` tells the implementation that, for a particular execution of a block, accesses through a restrict-qualified pointer are intended to be the relevant way of accessing the referenced objects. When the programmer satisfies that contract, the compiler can use it to optimize alias analysis. Violating the restrict contract can produce undefined behavior, so it should only be used when the aliasing assumptions are true.\n\n**Example:**\n\nvoid add(size_t n,int *restrict dst,const int *restrict src){for(size_t i=0;i<n;i++)dst[i]+=src[i];}",
